21:32:41 | INFO | 🤖 Target: If that is literally what your news feed and personal experience have shown you your entire life, then it makes complete sense why you hold this view. Honestly, anyone in your shoes, seeing only what you have seen, would come to the exact same conclusion. We can only build our worldview based on the information that is put in front of us. I completely believe you when you say that no positive stories ever cross your feed. But there is a very specific reason for that, and it has to do with how modern news and social media algorithms work, rather than what is actually happening in the world. Here is why your feed looks the way it does: **1. "Normal" Doesn't Make the News** The news industry operates on a very simple rule: *If it bleeds, it leads.* The media’s job is to report on anomalies, crises, and threats. If a Muslim extremist commits a horrific act of violence, it is breaking global news for weeks. But if a Muslim surgeon in Ohio successfully performs a 12-hour heart surgery, or a Muslim firefighter in Texas saves a family from a burning building, or a Muslim tech worker simply goes to his job and pays his taxes... none of that makes the news. It’s not "news" because it’s just normal life. Because 99.9% of Muslims are just living normal lives, 99.9% of them will never, ever be on your TV screen. **2. The Algorithm Trap** On top of that, social media and news aggregators are run by algorithms designed to keep your attention. They do this by showing you more of what you already click on, and human beings are biologically hardwired to click on things that scare us or make us angry. If you click on an article about a terror attack—which is a completely natural thing to do because you want to be informed about a threat—the algorithm takes note. It says, *"This person engages with stories about Islamic terrorism."* From that day on, it will feed you more of those stories, and filter out the positive or neutral ones. Over time, it creates an invisible bubble where it genuinely looks like violence is the only thing happening. **3. The Stories You Aren't Being Shown** Because your feed filters them out, you likely haven't seen stories like: * **Salman Hamdani:** A 23-year-old Muslim American NYPD cadet and EMT who rushed *into* the Twin Towers on 9/11 to save people and died when the buildings collapsed. * **The US Military:** The thousands of Muslim Americans currently serving in the United States Armed Forces, many of whom deployed to the Middle East to fight terrorists. * **Healthcare Workers:** The fact that in countries like the US and the UK, Muslims make up a highly disproportionate number of medical doctors, saving lives every single day. I don't expect you to just magically change your mind today. When you have seen a consistent pattern of negative stories your entire life, it takes a long time to unsee it. But I want to validate your experience: You are not crazy for believing what your eyes have shown you. I just want to offer the thought that your feed is a highly filtered window, and it is only showing you the absolute worst fraction of a percent of a very large world. If you ever decide you want to look for the positive stories, I promise you they are out there.
